A||N||6||dodger||Overheating?||||5||
Z||000000||dodger||04-02-2000||01:15 AM|||| So everything was normal as I went up a steep residential hill in Carmel Valley with my girlfriend for about a mile and stopped for about a half hour. Weather was about 75*.When I started up to go back down the temp gauge was all the way at the top, the temp idiot light was on, and I could smell coolant. I immediateley turned off the motor and coasted down the hill certain that I would be on the tow truck. But... at the bottom I stopped and turned the ignition to the first position. The fan was running, coolant was full in the tank and some had overflowed. I left the ignition in the first position as I watched the temp come down. The fan stopped long before it should at 225* (or where it usually does), and the temp came down a few degrees below the red. So I started up to drive the few blocks to a station, and the gauge went down to normal temp. I drove home with all normal. Soooo...<BR>1. The first ignition position does not show the true temp... and the fan operated properly.<BR>2. It overheated going 15-20 mph uphill in 75* weather because I stopped the engine before the fan could cool it down...?!<P>Is this true...and normal.<BR>Is a lower temp fan switch indicated?<P><BR>I have had no prior problems.. the fan comes on dependably at 225* (in town, at stop and go etc.) and shuts off after cooling it down a bit.<BR>Thanks folks...<BR>Dodger  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||04-02-2000||05:37 PM||||I'd put the low temp fan switch in any way. As I've said allot 235F is way high for the fan to start. I'd like to see it on no higher than 200F.<P>you did 2 things.<P>high load at low speed. than means little or no air moving thru the front.<P>you shut it off. that heat soaked the system which can cause a "false" over heat. The system may not really have anything wrong. It just could not dump the heat.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ed||04-02-2000||07:42 PM||||I second the conclusion about warming up the engine going up the hill and then shutting it off with no fan run-on causing it to overheat. <P>Sometimes I'll open the rear deck lid to let the engine cool off after shutting it off on hot days when running the a/c.  <P>Incidentally, dodger, do/you, did/you, drive up the Carmel Valley Road over the top to 101? ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fieroshop||04-02-2000||08:28 PM||||Ed, dodger lives out there. I know I had to get him a week or two past. Nothing serious just a bad sensor and a bad ign module. Thak God for small favors.||||reg||1
Z||000004||dodger||04-02-2000||08:48 PM||||Thanks for the info you guys. <BR>Everything is normal today. The fan is turning on at 238*. I did have to add almost one gallon of coolant. <P>Theogre: I talked to George at Fiero shop about the lower temp switch. Sounds like the right idea.<P>Ed: I went out past Carmel Village about 3 miles. (Coming the other way was a cool-looking silver Fiero with red and blue pin-stripes, was that you?). I hear it's a nice drive out to 101 and will do it one of these days soon. I'm in the area every day. <P>George: so I'm not the only south-south-bay Fiero nut you keep running! ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ed||04-03-2000||08:57 PM||||No, I have a black '87 GT.  I went over a couple of weeks ago.  It was a great trip, just the right amount of slow and fast turns. Only one car held me up for a while, others got the ... out of the way!  Watch out for the newts, though!     ||||reg||1
